item.text:
Debug it along the MOOS app data path, in this order:

1. Confirm the app is running and iterating

In `uXMS`, first check that the process is alive and publishing normal app status variables such as:

```text
APPCAST
PROC_WATCH_SUMMARY
DB_CLIENTS
```

Then add temporary output in `Iterate()`:

```cpp
bool pOdometry::Iterate()
{
  AppCastingMOOSApp::Iterate();

  std::cout << "Iterate() called" << std::endl;

  AppCastingMOOSApp::PostReport();
  return true;
}
```

If you never see this, the app may not be launched, may not be connected to the MOOSDB, or its `AppTick` may be wrong or missing.

In the `.moos` file, check:

```text
ProcessConfig = pOdometry
{
  AppTick   = 4
  CommsTick = 4
}
```

`AppTick` controls how often `Iterate()` runs. If `Iterate()` is where `ODOMETRY_DIST` is computed or published, no `Iterate()` means no odometry output.

2. Confirm `OnStartUp()` registers subscriptions

Your app only receives mail for variables it registers for. In `OnStartUp()`, make sure you call your registration function after reading config:

```cpp
bool pOdometry::OnStartUp()
{
  AppCastingMOOSApp::OnStartUp();

  // read mission parameters here

  RegisterVariables();
  return true;
}
```

Then in `RegisterVariables()`:

```cpp
void pOdometry::RegisterVariables()
{
  AppCastingMOOSApp::RegisterVariables();
  Register("NAV_X", 0);
  Register("NAV_Y", 0);
}
```

Common mistakes:

```cpp
Register("NAV_X", 0);
Register("NAV_Y", 0);
```

must use the exact MOOS variable names being published. If the vehicle publishes `NAV_X`/`NAV_Y`, do not subscribe to `X`, `Y`, `NAV_LAT`, etc.

3. Confirm mail is arriving in `OnNewMail()`

Add direct instrumentation:

```cpp
bool pOdometry::OnNewMail(MOOSMSG_LIST &NewMail)
{
  AppCastingMOOSApp::OnNewMail(NewMail);

  for (MOOSMSG_LIST::iterator p = NewMail.begin(); p != NewMail.end(); ++p) {
    CMOOSMsg &msg = *p;
    std::string key = msg.GetKey();

    std::cout << "MAIL: " << key;

    if (msg.IsDouble())
      std::cout << " = " << msg.GetDouble();
    else if (msg.IsString())
      std::cout << " = " << msg.GetString();

    std::cout << std::endl;

    if (key == "NAV_X") {
      m_current_x = msg.GetDouble();
      m_got_x = true;
    } else if (key == "NAV_Y") {
      m_current_y = msg.GetDouble();
      m_got_y = true;
    }
  }

  return true;
}
```

If `OnNewMail()` is called but you never see `NAV_X` or `NAV_Y`, the problem is subscription or upstream publication.

Use `uXMS` to check the upstream variables directly:

```text
uXMS NAV_X NAV_Y
```

If `NAV_X` and `NAV_Y` are not changing there, `pOdometry` cannot compute distance.

4. Confirm your odometry logic has initialized state

A common bug is waiting forever because the first position was never accepted as the baseline.

Typical pattern:

```cpp
if (!m_initialized && m_got_x && m_got_y) {
  m_previous_x = m_current_x;
  m_previous_y = m_current_y;
  m_total_distance = 0;
  m_initialized = true;
  return true;
}
```

Then on later updates:

```cpp
double dx = m_current_x - m_previous_x;
double dy = m_current_y - m_previous_y;
double delta = hypot(dx, dy);

m_total_distance += delta;

m_previous_x = m_current_x;
m_previous_y = m_current_y;
```

Make sure you do not require both `NAV_X` and `NAV_Y` to arrive in the same mail batch. They may arrive separately.

5. Confirm `Notify()` is actually called

Wherever the distance is computed, add a temporary print immediately before publishing:

```cpp
std::cout << "Notify ODOMETRY_DIST = " << m_total_distance << std::endl;
Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", m_total_distance);
```

If you see the print but not the variable in `uXMS`, check for a spelling mismatch:

```text
ODOMETRY_DIST
```

not:

```text
ODOMETRY_DISTANCE
ODOM_DIST
ODOMETRY
```

Also check that you are watching the correct MOOSDB and community.

6. Watch the exact output variable

Run:

```text
uXMS ODOMETRY_DIST NAV_X NAV_Y
```

If `NAV_X` and `NAV_Y` are changing but `ODOMETRY_DIST` is absent, the bug is inside `pOdometry`.

If `ODOMETRY_DIST` appears once and then stops, check whether `Iterate()` is still running and whether your code only calls `Notify()` under a condition that becomes false.

7. Use AppCasting instead of only `cout`

For a MOOS-IvP app, add internal state to the appcast report:

```cpp
bool pOdometry::buildReport()
{
  m_msgs << "Current X: " << m_current_x << std::endl;
  m_msgs << "Current Y: " << m_current_y << std::endl;
  m_msgs << "Previous X: " << m_previous_x << std::endl;
  m_msgs << "Previous Y: " << m_previous_y << std::endl;
  m_msgs << "Total Distance: " << m_total_distance << std::endl;
  m_msgs << "Got X: " << m_got_x << std::endl;
  m_msgs << "Got Y: " << m_got_y << std::endl;
  m_msgs << "Initialized: " << m_initialized << std::endl;
  return true;
}
```

Then inspect with appcasting tools instead of relying only on terminal output.

The most likely failures are:

```text
OnStartUp() never calls RegisterVariables()
RegisterVariables() does not subscribe to NAV_X/NAV_Y
NAV_X/NAV_Y are not being published upstream
OnNewMail() handles the wrong variable names
Iterate() is not running because AppTick/config/launch is wrong
Notify() is never reached due to initialization logic
Notify() publishes a misspelled variable name
uXMS is connected to the wrong MOOSDB/community
```

A minimal good flow is:

```cpp
OnStartUp()
  -> RegisterVariables()
      -> Register("NAV_X", 0)
      -> Register("NAV_Y", 0)

OnNewMail()
  -> update latest NAV_X/NAV_Y
  -> mark data as received

Iterate()
  -> if initialized, compute distance
  -> Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", m_total_distance)
```

Start by proving each stage with temporary prints or appcast fields: subscribed, received mail, iterating, computed value, notified value.
